  "mechanisms": [
    "Curvilinear forms elicit pleasant emotions, which drive aesthetic preference.",
    "The anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) mediates the reward and emotional salience associated with curvilinear forms.",
    "Habituation to rectilinear forms in daily life may reduce their threat signaling (amygdala activation).",
    "A distributed brain network, including frontopolar cortex, superior frontal gyrus, globus pallidus, precuneus, parahippocampus, and middle occipital gyrus, underlies aesthetic assessment of visual objects.",
    "Pleasantness and beauty judgments activate largely dissociable aspects of a common aesthetic network.",
    "Visual cortex activation for curvilinear spaces during approach-avoidance decisions suggests enhanced visual processing for these forms in a behavioral context."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Context (e.g., positive vs. negative emotional valence for curved objects)",
    "Exposure duration (for amygdala response to rectilinear stimuli)",
    "Expertise (architects vs. non-architects)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Designing curvilinear spaces may enhance aesthetic judgments and emotional responses, potentially leading to more pleasant work and life spaces.",
    "The findings underscore the importance of emotion in architectural preference."
  ],
  "domains": [
